Changelog: 24-10-2026 19:49:22

Release 1.0.0.

Changelog: 24-10-2026 18:16:35

Added. The pinned RPCS3 moves to build 0.0.43-20395 (46b7fb67, 24-10-2026; owner, 24-10-2026: the first pin
after 19-10-2026, when RPCS3 began to re-attach a controller that reconnects with a new device path: a Bluetooth
controller switched off during a game and woken again is read again). Its AppImage is 83,180,532 bytes
(SHA-256 9df18bd0…817a5c), checked before it is installed; "about 80 MB" in the wiki. The headless package
install was tried with it on the board as with 20133 (a dummy package: no window, the lines in RPCS3's log). The
board test with a game is the owner's.

Added. The owner's remarks after the package install and a game (24-10-2026). Games Install no longer opens
RPCS3's window: RPCS3 installs the packages headless (--headless --installpkg, which this build supports; proved
on the board with a dummy package), once per file, as the window's job off the GUI thread with the pills disabled
meanwhile: the .rap and .edat licenses first (RPCS3 copies them into its exdata), then the packages in the app's
order, the games and DLC by name and the updates last, read from each package's unencrypted header (the PATCH
flag in its package type; RPCS3's own check refuses an update whose game is not installed, and one run of RPCS3
with the whole folder takes its files in name order and stops at the first failure, an update's name sorting
before its game's). RPCS3's exit code says nothing (the build ends a headless run with an error of its own once
the work is done), so the result comes from RPCS3's own log (~/.cache/rpcs3/RPCS3.log: the titles installed, the
packages it could not install, the files that are no package, the licenses copied), and the games and licenses
that came are counted when the log says nothing. Only the folder's own files are taken, as RPCS3 takes them. The
state lines (owner): Disc games shows the folders' names alone, each a link that takes the folder out after a
question (the paths and the count in the tooltip; Remove a folder… went); PKG games shows RPCS3's games folder as
dev_hdd0/game/ while a game is installed and — while none is, the count and what Install takes in the tooltip.
The Settings tab's Sound box shows Bazzite's volume for RPCS3, the one its sound settings remember per application
(WirePlumber's stream-properties, read only; the owner found RPCS3 muted there): Muted or 0 % in red, said once
in the status line and the log. The controller tooltip and the wiki say that a controller switched off during a
game (USB HD Control's idle timer did it on the board, after 10 minutes) is not picked up again by this RPCS3
build when it comes back: RPCS3 matches a known controller by name, device path and serial, and a Bluetooth
reconnect brings a new path; RPCS3 changed this on 19-10-2026, after the pinned build (30-09-2026). The run
wrapper knows --headless (no game logged) and lists it in its help. Tests: the headless install with a fake RPCS3
(the order, the log read, nothing while RPCS3 runs), the package header (the first bytes of the owner's three
Lumines Supernova packages as fixtures), the install order, the report from RPCS3's log, the saved volume, the
Sound row, the state lines, the folder link (No keeps it), the wrapper and the installer test. The reading of
Bazzite's volume moved to core (sound.py) the same day, for every manager; the Sound row reads the same.

Changelog: 24-10-2026 15:22:00

Added. The owner's remarks on the rebuilt window and the round from scratch (24-10-2026). Games Install, a pill in
place of Games Remove folder: it takes the folder with your .pkg files and hands it to RPCS3 (--installpkg with the
folder, in RPCS3's own window), which installs every package below it and copies the .rap and .edat licenses beside
them into its exdata; the window follows RPCS3's games and licenses until they change, as it follows the firmware,
and says what was installed. Remove a folder is a link in the Disc games line. The state lines are short (owner):
RPCS3 shows the version alone, the build and a newer landmark in the tooltip (the newer one also in the log once);
Firmware the version; Disc games the folders with the number of disc games found (or that RPCS3 knows); PKG games
the number installed in RPCS3. A Screen setting column in the table, as PCSX2 Manager's: 16:9 (RPCS3's default),
4:3 or Fill screen, written into the game's own settings in RPCS3 (custom_configs/config_<Id>.yml, the two Video
lines, the rest of a file RPCS3 wrote kept), not while RPCS3 runs. The app sets RPCS3 up while it is installed and
not running (userdata.py, as PCSX2 Manager's pcsx2_settings): RPCS3's welcome dialog off and its own update check
off in GuiConfigs/CurrentSettings.ini (the dialog waited for a tick in "I have read the Quickstart guide" before
RPCS3's window opened; the update replaced the installed build with a newer one on the board, after which RPCS3's
Settings asked to remove "obsolete settings", since the two builds know different settings), and player 1 (and 2)
on the controllers connected in input_configs/global/Default.yml, through RPCS3's SDL handler with RPCS3's own
default mapping: the round found RPCS3 playing with the keyboard, its pad file empty. RPCS3 knows a controller by
SDL's name, so the app asks the system's SDL3 (Bazzite's, the library RPCS3 builds in) in a process of its own
whenever the controllers connected change, and falls back on the kernel's name without it; the file starts with a
comment of the app's and a pad file RPCS3's own Pads settings wrote is never touched. The hint under the table
links to Steam (steam://open/games: Steam's library, Steam started when it does not run). The run wrapper knows
--installpkg (no game logged for it) and lists it in its help. Fixed. The Refresh pill had another background
than the boxes beside it when the window opened: it holds the keyboard focus then, and the focus style painted a
translucent tint over the window instead of the pill's sheen (seen by the owner in PCSX2 Manager, the same here);
the focus now changes nothing and the mouse's tint is the sheen with the blue mixed in. Tests: the set-up and the
pad file (RPCS3's own left alone, the kernel's names without SDL3), the packages and the watch, the screen column,
the Steam link, the short state lines, the look (the focused Refresh pill's pixels against the box beside it), the
SDL probe, the wrapper's --installpkg (also in the installer test). The wiki says it all.

Added. A Settings tab, as PCSX2 Manager's (owner, 24-10-2026, after the screenshots: the picture settings PCSX2
Manager has, at least): the picture (the renderer, the resolution scale, anisotropic filtering, MSAA, VSync, the
frame limit, output scaling, Fill the screen as the default for games without a Screen setting), the emulation
(the PPU and SPU decoders), the sound (the output library, the volume up to 200 %, buffering) and the messages in
a game (trophies, shader compilation), each one line of RPCS3's config.yml (rpcs3_settings.py, the values and
defaults from RPCS3 0.0.43's source), written the moment it is changed while RPCS3 does not run, every other line
kept; a value the app does not know is shown as it is (settings_page.py). With the GUI settings the app now also
chooses RPCS3's dark style once, while none is chosen and the suite's theme is dark: the welcome dialog, now off,
would have ticked it. Tests: the model (defaults, a line in a file RPCS3 wrote, the values RPCS3 spells), the tab
(a change, an unknown value, nothing while RPCS3 runs, a widget per kind) and the window's tab following the file.
Quitting a game on the controller (owner, 24-10-2026, asked for Options + Share as PCSX2 Manager's Select +
Start): RPCS3 has no chord that quits a game outright; its home menu, with Exit Game in it, is the pad's way out,
and the mapping the app writes opens it with Share + Options together (RPCS3's default for the PS Button,
"Back&Start,Guide") or the Guide button. The controller's tooltip and the wiki say so.

